= Group cognition — Simple =

'''Group cognition''' studies how thinking happens through interaction among members of a group.

A group can:

* share information;
* divide expertise;
* build common understanding;
* solve problems;
* remember through shared documents;
* coordinate action.

The important idea is that the process can exist at the level of interaction, not only inside separate individuals.

This does **not** mean the group has one literal brain or consciousness.

== Shared mental models ==

Teams often work better when members have similar expectations about important parts of the task.

A '''shared mental model''' can include common understanding of:

* goals;
* roles;
* procedures;
* equipment;
* likely events.

Shared understanding can reduce the need for constant explanation.

But a team can share the same incorrect belief.

Agreement is not the same as accuracy.

== Transactive memory ==

Groups do not need everyone to know everything.

A '''transactive memory system''' means that members know who has which expertise.

For example:

<pre>
Ana knows the database.
Ben knows the hardware.
Cara knows who to ask about both.
</pre>

Kyle Lewis developed a measure of transactive memory based on specialization, credibility, and coordination.<ref name="lewis">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12940401/ Lewis, K. (2003), “Measuring transactive memory systems in the field.”]</ref>

== Common ground ==

[[Common ground]] is information that people treat as mutually shared during communication.

If two people use the word “server,” they need enough shared understanding to know what system they mean.

When common ground is missing, people ask questions, repeat information, or correct misunderstandings.

This process is called '''grounding'''.

== Interaction can create new ideas ==

A group discussion can produce a solution that no member had before.

One person proposes an idea.

Another finds a problem.

A third adds evidence.

The first person revises the idea.

The final result emerges through the sequence.

Gerry Stahl uses “group cognition” for this kind of collaborative knowledge building.<ref name="stahl">[https://mitpress.mit.edu/9780262195393/group-cognition/ Stahl, G. (2006), ''Group Cognition''.]</ref>

== Interactive team cognition ==

Nancy Cooke and colleagues argue that team cognition should be studied as an activity occurring through interaction, not only as shared knowledge stored in team members' heads.<ref name="cooke">[https://doi.org/10.1111/cogs.12009 Cooke et al. (2013), “Interactive Team Cognition.”]</ref>

This approach emphasizes:

* dynamic communication;
* coordination;
* context;
* adaptation.

== Communication is not always helpful ==

More communication can help share unique information.

It can also create:

* interruption;
* overload;
* conformity;
* repetition.

A strong team communicates what is needed rather than maximizing the number of messages.

== Hidden information ==

Groups sometimes fail even when they collectively have all the needed information.

In hidden-profile experiments, different members receive different evidence.

Groups often spend more time discussing facts everyone already knows than facts only one person knows.

Important information can therefore remain hidden inside the group.

== Shared tools and artifacts ==

Groups think with:

* whiteboards;
* documents;
* diagrams;
* issue trackers;
* spreadsheets;
* code repositories.

These artifacts preserve information and make coordination easier.

A shared document can be part of the group cognitive process without being a mind.

== Group cognition versus collective intelligence ==

[[Collective intelligence]] asks how well a group performs.

Group cognition asks how the group thinks and coordinates.

A crowd can produce an accurate average without talking to each other.

That can be collective intelligence with little group cognition.

A team can have complex discussion and still reach the wrong answer.

That is group cognition without high collective intelligence.

== Human–AI teams ==

AI systems can participate by searching, summarizing, monitoring, or proposing ideas.

Humans need an accurate understanding of what the AI can and cannot do.

If a team trusts an AI system too much, one model error can spread through the whole group.

